1
00:00:13,732 --> 00:00:18,476
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is the note on attaka-vacara, the first of the shorter notes.

2
00:00:19,997 --> 00:00:24,820
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes translated as unattainable by reasoning or not accessible to doubt.

3
00:00:25,381 --> 00:00:30,665
[SPEAKER_01]: But the Cartesian cogito ergo sum is also, in a sense, inaccessible to doubt.

4
00:00:31,405 --> 00:00:34,788
[SPEAKER_01]: For I cannot doubt my existence without tacitly assuming it.

5
00:00:35,529 --> 00:00:41,473
[SPEAKER_01]: This merely shows, however, that one cannot get beyond the cogito by doubting it.

6
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:44,061
[SPEAKER_01]: The dhamma is beyond the cogito.

7
00:00:44,941 --> 00:00:47,322
[SPEAKER_01]: The cogito, then, can be reached by doubt.

8
00:00:47,962 --> 00:00:54,385
[SPEAKER_01]: One doubts and doubts until one finds what one cannot doubt, what is inaccessible to doubt, namely the cogito.

9
00:00:55,466 --> 00:00:57,406
[SPEAKER_01]: But the dhamma cannot be reached this way.

10
00:00:58,067 --> 00:01:02,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, the dhamma, though certainly inaccessible to doubt, is more than that.

11
00:01:03,009 --> 00:01:05,490
[SPEAKER_01]: It is altogether beyond the sphere of doubt.

12
00:01:06,353 --> 00:01:19,200
[SPEAKER_01]: The rationalist, however, does not even reach the inadequate cogito, or if he does reach it, he overshoots the mark, for he starts from the axiom that everything can be doubted, including, of course, the cogito.

13
00:01:20,441 --> 00:01:20,861
So...

14
00:01:26,285 --> 00:01:31,307
[SPEAKER_01]: What, what, what, what is it exactly about the dhamma that makes it beyond that doubt?

15
00:01:31,467 --> 00:01:38,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, I can doubt, oh, what is, what is, what are the five aggregates?

16
00:01:38,389 --> 00:01:39,650
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the cause of suffering?

17
00:01:39,730 --> 00:01:41,851
[SPEAKER_01]: It seems like I can doubt these things.

18
00:01:42,511 --> 00:01:50,034
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, here, Yanavira is saying that, no, the dhamma is not something within that sphere of doubtful thinking at all.

19
00:01:51,434 --> 00:01:54,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, what it means, means the dhamma goes beyond that sphere.

20
00:01:56,605 --> 00:02:06,273
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, even your experience of doubt is within that universal sphere that dhamma describes.

21
00:02:07,134 --> 00:02:11,177
[SPEAKER_02]: And the reason why you cannot doubt that universal sphere is because that's what you are.

22
00:02:11,597 --> 00:02:13,018
[SPEAKER_02]: That is the structure of your experience.

23
00:02:13,039 --> 00:02:14,079
[SPEAKER_02]: That is your experience.

24
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:20,845
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can step back through doubting only so far, as Gojjito Ergosum describes, where you can then doubt

25
00:02:21,335 --> 00:02:23,317
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, where you cannot doubt the fact that you're doubting.

26
00:02:23,637 --> 00:02:26,760
[SPEAKER_02]: You can doubt the content of your doubt, but you cannot doubt the nature of the doubt.

27
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:28,021
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what dhamma is.

28
00:02:28,041 --> 00:02:37,129
[SPEAKER_02]: The nature of phenomena present, being... that phenomena might be doubt, might be a feeling of happiness, might be... whatever.

29
00:02:37,589 --> 00:02:40,932
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever has a nature to appear, is... can be called phenomena.

30
00:02:42,113 --> 00:02:46,016
[SPEAKER_02]: So the dhamma is not concerned with the content of that phenomena, which is what you doubt.

31
00:02:46,657 --> 00:02:49,339
[SPEAKER_02]: It's concerned with the nature, which is what you cannot doubt.

32
00:02:50,235 --> 00:02:51,996
[SPEAKER_02]: The nature of an arisen thing.

33
00:02:52,777 --> 00:02:57,060
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why he said, the Buddha said... How did he put it?

34
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:00,003
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I don't want to misparaphrase, but...

35
00:03:01,844 --> 00:03:02,545
[SPEAKER_02]: He...

36
00:03:02,605 --> 00:03:07,228
[SPEAKER_02]: The Tathagata teaches the understanding and freedom from... Well, I am paraphrasing, obviously.

37
00:03:07,849 --> 00:03:12,152
[SPEAKER_02]: Freedom from presently arisen things.

38
00:03:12,292 --> 00:03:16,035
[SPEAKER_02]: Means that things in the utmost universal nature

39
00:03:16,797 --> 00:03:26,380
[SPEAKER_02]: They can vary by the content, but what they cannot vary in, is the fact that they have a nature of arising, as in being manifested.

40
00:03:27,560 --> 00:03:32,242
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what's in common to everything that you experience, is that it has arisen, it has presented itself.

41
00:03:33,322 --> 00:03:34,502
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's something you cannot doubt.

42
00:03:34,562 --> 00:03:34,742
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

43
00:03:34,782 --> 00:03:39,824
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the doubt is an experience in itself, that shares the nature of being presented in itself.

44
00:03:41,201 --> 00:03:55,187
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why if somebody is able to discern that, you actually free yourself from all the passion towards the content, aversion towards the content of your experience, hatred, distraction, delusion, doubt in itself as well.

45
00:03:55,247 --> 00:03:56,548
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot doubt the dhamma then.

46
00:03:56,568 --> 00:04:04,311
[SPEAKER_02]: You can doubt various other things as an experience of doubt, but you cannot doubt the nature of manifestation of that experience.

47
00:04:05,292 --> 00:04:07,052
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's a completely the opposite direction.

48
00:04:07,112 --> 00:04:10,494
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it's not even the opposite direction, it's a vertical direction, as Enavira would refer to it.

49
00:04:12,805 --> 00:04:19,169
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it's inaccessible by reasoning, because reasoning operates linearly, operates in space and time.

50
00:04:20,790 --> 00:04:28,895
[SPEAKER_02]: But in order to operate your reasoning in space and time, the phenomenon of space and time needs to be there, present, manifested.

51
00:04:29,456 --> 00:04:34,659
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's where the dhamma pertains, to understanding of that structure, not being concerned with the content.

52
00:04:35,282 --> 00:04:44,528
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what the Buddha would say when he would... Five Aggregates, for example, is a way of describing your experience as a whole, in the utmost universal manner.

53
00:04:45,828 --> 00:05:00,297
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why he would say, whatever feeling there is, past, future, present, unpleasant, pleasant, neutral, big or small, far or near, personal, impersonal, internal, external, doesn't matter.

54
00:05:00,517 --> 00:05:03,719
[SPEAKER_02]: What matters is, a feeling is there, hence,

55
00:05:04,172 --> 00:05:05,892
[SPEAKER_02]: The aggregate of feeling is present.

56
00:05:06,533 --> 00:05:07,713
[SPEAKER_02]: And the same goes for everything.

57
00:05:08,293 --> 00:05:10,414
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter what perception is present.

58
00:05:10,434 --> 00:05:18,056
[SPEAKER_02]: Whether it's blue, yellow, white, red, past, future, present, mine, not mine, impersonal, personal.

59
00:05:18,076 --> 00:05:19,876
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter the content of it.

60
00:05:20,216 --> 00:05:23,097
[SPEAKER_02]: What matters is that the phenomenon of perception is present.

61
00:05:23,157 --> 00:05:24,497
[SPEAKER_02]: The nature of perception is there.

62
00:05:24,537 --> 00:05:28,158
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, the aggregate of perception is manifested.

63
00:05:28,779 --> 00:05:33,880
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why the thinking... And again, it comes back to that negative thinking we discussed in the previous video.

64
00:05:35,042 --> 00:05:44,730
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why thinking needs to stop being concerned with a method and a content and trying to figure it out on that basis, but start seeing the peripheral of your content.

65
00:05:45,230 --> 00:05:46,871
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you develop mindfulness as well.

66
00:05:47,652 --> 00:05:50,154
[SPEAKER_02]: Mindfulness to the extent necessary for the final knowledge.

67
00:05:50,895 --> 00:06:01,643
[SPEAKER_02]: As into the peripheral to the extent necessary to actually discern the whole human situation without resulting to greed, aversion, delusion that arise as a result of not understanding it.

68
00:06:05,401 --> 00:06:10,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever doubt arises, it always presents itself as promising.

69
00:06:10,886 --> 00:06:12,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, we follow it...

70
00:06:12,587 --> 00:06:14,829
[SPEAKER_02]: It presses you towards the content of your doubt.

71
00:06:15,290 --> 00:06:16,551
[SPEAKER_02]: It presses you to clarify.

72
00:06:16,791 --> 00:06:24,798
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why doubt is a hindrance that pulls you, that makes you kind of revolve around it, but failing to see that it's... well, it's like a trap.

73
00:06:24,958 --> 00:06:27,100
[SPEAKER_02]: It traps you, it hooks you in, pulls you in.

74
00:06:27,925 --> 00:06:46,669
[SPEAKER_02]: And you're being concerned with the content of the doubt for the sake of dispelling the doubt, failing to see that the only true way to dispel the doubt is to see the doubt as a phenomenon that has arisen, as an experience that manifests, pertains to the general nature of the manifestation of experience as a whole.

75
00:06:47,349 --> 00:06:53,570
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means that you might doubt the content, but the experience of doubt is present and you cannot doubt that.

76
00:06:54,561 --> 00:07:23,003
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you overcome your emotionally charged assumption in regard to the presence of phenomena, as in you overcome your appropriation of it, this is mine, then you won't be pulled into the content of the doubt, nor will you be pulled into the content of your sensual thoughts, nor will you be pulled into the content of the aversive thoughts, because you have actually understood the structure of it and by doing so you freed yourself from the

77
00:07:25,438 --> 00:07:38,089
[SPEAKER_01]: So, one's trying to first approach the suttas or the notes or whatever.

78
00:07:38,990 --> 00:07:44,995
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't necessarily have a wide range of, you know, familiarity with all the terms.

79
00:07:45,255 --> 00:07:47,657
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, inevitably, they'll be doubtful and confused.

80
00:07:49,335 --> 00:08:00,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Is what you're saying that they shouldn't necessarily give in to that kind of pressing need to solve the doubt, as opposed to just kind of including the doubt in whatever they're thinking?

81
00:08:00,441 --> 00:08:04,223
[SPEAKER_02]: Eventually, yes, but initially, the person won't be able to help it.

82
00:08:04,363 --> 00:08:05,683
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the manner of thinking, but...

83
00:08:05,984 --> 00:08:08,345
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's going to be unpleasant.

84
00:08:10,126 --> 00:08:18,730
[SPEAKER_02]: Constantly having doubt arising and questioning your whole existence, not just your scholastic point of view, it's going to be unpleasant, but that's... it's a...

85
00:08:19,678 --> 00:08:23,359
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a necessary thing to go through.

86
00:08:23,419 --> 00:08:40,686
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, eventually, of course, when you start discerning the nature of the present phenomena, doesn't matter what it is, even if it's a doubt in itself, you will recognize that all you need to do is stay on the... continue discerning the extent of that presence of that phenomena, not being concerned with the content of it.

87
00:08:41,226 --> 00:08:43,007
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how you overcome the hindrances as well.

88
00:08:43,327 --> 00:08:44,407
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the first jhāna.

89
00:08:44,907 --> 00:08:47,028
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, if you fully develop that kind of thinking,

90
00:08:47,766 --> 00:08:53,748
[SPEAKER_02]: That is free from the pressure of the content, of sensuality, five hindrances in general.

91
00:08:54,669 --> 00:09:02,312
[SPEAKER_02]: That is the thinking and pondering of the first jhāna, vittāka-vicāra, the correct phenomenological thinking that has no greed, aversion, delusion in them.

92
00:09:02,352 --> 00:09:03,812
[SPEAKER_02]: They have been subdued beforehand.

93
00:09:05,653 --> 00:09:09,455
[SPEAKER_01]: The same as overcoming the fetter of doubt as well?

94
00:09:09,715 --> 00:09:15,257
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, the fetter of doubt, it's more fundamental in a sense of, like, not even discerning

95
00:09:16,040 --> 00:09:17,402
[SPEAKER_02]: So, more fundamental.

96
00:09:34,412 --> 00:09:39,996
[SPEAKER_02]: So, a sotāpanna, obviously, is free from the factor of the doubt, but he can still be assailed by the hindrance of the doubt.

97
00:09:40,436 --> 00:09:48,222
[SPEAKER_02]: Not in regard to the fundamental aspects of the dhamma that he understood, but in regard to other things, because doubt in itself, it's unpleasant.

98
00:09:48,262 --> 00:09:49,103
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why people don't like it.

99
00:09:49,123 --> 00:09:54,227
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why people give in to it and try to get rid of it through clarifying or defining whatever they're doubting about.

100
00:09:55,508 --> 00:09:57,869
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes, that is necessary.

101
00:09:57,889 --> 00:10:02,793
[SPEAKER_02]: To a degree, you must look into what you're doubting, but there is a clear point where you can recognize

102
00:10:03,197 --> 00:10:09,099
[SPEAKER_02]: When that doubt becomes almost neurotic, when that doubt becomes sort of self-maintaining, it's just there for the purpose of the doubt.

103
00:10:09,699 --> 00:10:12,580
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not concerned with any evidence or proof that you're giving it.

104
00:10:12,660 --> 00:10:13,901
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just not disappearing.

105
00:10:13,941 --> 00:10:20,423
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's when you're acting out of your doubt emotionally, like through through emotional response, not through like trying to understand the nature of that doubt.

106
00:10:21,283 --> 00:10:24,264
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's something that you, a person, will need to be restrained in regard to.

107
00:10:26,405 --> 00:10:27,425
[SPEAKER_02]: Feeling it emotionally.

108
00:10:28,412 --> 00:10:33,933
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the same... it's that same restraint that helps one then overcome doubt eventually.

109
00:10:34,113 --> 00:10:36,274
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, with... it's not just only like restraining the sensuality.

110
00:10:36,294 --> 00:10:36,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, absolutely.

111
00:10:36,894 --> 00:10:45,476
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you... if you have no restraint, if you're not able to withhold, withstand the doubt and let it endure, you... you will be giving in whenever it arises.

112
00:10:45,496 --> 00:10:48,336
[SPEAKER_02]: That pressure, whenever it manifests, you'll be pulled by it, you'll be pressured by it.

113
00:10:49,136 --> 00:10:56,898
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's then... you can't... how can you then see the universal nature of that phenomena behind its content, when you're fully absorbing the content right away?

114
00:10:57,818 --> 00:10:58,538
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, you can't help it.

115
00:11:00,159 --> 00:11:01,740
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where restraint comes first.

116
00:11:01,941 --> 00:11:05,163
[SPEAKER_02]: Sīla comes first, because it's the same principle of sensuality and your will.

117
00:11:05,623 --> 00:11:10,586
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to be able to allow it to arise as a thought, and then recognize that that's not what sensuality is.

118
00:11:11,046 --> 00:11:13,908
[SPEAKER_02]: It's your craving, delight, welcoming, entertaining that thought.

119
00:11:14,028 --> 00:11:14,969
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what sensuality is.

120
00:11:15,369 --> 00:11:25,616
[SPEAKER_02]: But you will never be able to see that for as long as you're not restrained physically and verbally in regard to sensuality, and your automatic response to a reasoned sensuality is giving in to the pursuit of the content.

121
00:11:26,396 --> 00:11:27,617
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, in case of your will,

122
00:11:27,970 --> 00:11:30,594
[SPEAKER_02]: Give in towards the denial of the content.

123
00:11:32,197 --> 00:11:35,242
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't even see the nature behind that content, the peripheral.

